# NEXUS database migrations (NX-030)

Alembic, async, configured from `NEXUS_DATABASE_URL`. No connection string is
stored in `alembic.ini`.

## Commands

    make migrate                       # upgrade to head
    make migrate-down                  # step back one revision
    make revision m="add x"            # autogenerate a new revision
    uv run alembic current             # show the applied revision
    uv run alembic history --verbose   # show the chain

Migrations are a **deploy step, not an application startup step**. The service
does not create or alter schema on boot: several replicas starting at once would
race, and a failed migration would surface as a crash loop rather than a failed
deploy. `docker compose` runs a one-shot `migrate` service before `api` starts.

## Rollback

Every revision implements `downgrade()`, and `tests/test_migrations.py` asserts
that upgrade -> downgrade -> upgrade leaves the schema intact. Rollback is
rehearsed rather than assumed (delivery plan Phase 6; SDD 14).

Stepping back a revision that removed a column also discards what was in it.
Rolling back a *release* is routine; rolling back a *migration* against an
environment holding real data needs a backup taken first.

## The audit table grant (NX-022)

`audit_record` is append-only. On PostgreSQL, revision 0001 withdraws the
UPDATE and DELETE privileges from the application role, so the guarantee is
enforced by the database rather than by convention in the repository layer.
The role name comes from `NEXUS_DB_APP_ROLE` (default `nexus`).

SQLite has no privilege system, so the revision skips this step and the
guarantee rests on `AuditRepository` exposing no mutating method. That is
adequate for local development and is *not* adequate for the pilot - Gate 2
evidence must be taken from PostgreSQL.

A privileged operator retains the ability to remove audit rows. That is
deliberate: retention and legal hold need a path. It is a separate privileged
process, never the application role.
